why 1 3/4 SIX_FOUR Over the bass's held B♭ the upper voices move to an E♭ chord and back: a pedal ⁶₄ over the tonic. It decorates the bass note.
why 1 3/4 PROGRESSION The bass holds B♭ under this chord, so IV⁶₄ is a neighbor chord over a pedal, not a step backward from V.
why 2 1/4 DOUBLING The bass walks down B♭–A♭–G, and the diminished chord over its A♭ is a passing one, pulling toward E♭ (IV). Its doubled F follows the voices' lines.
why 2 1/2 PARALLEL_OCTAVES Alto and tenor move from a unison F to an octave B♭ in contrary motion, the alto up and the tenor down. Bach accepts octaves by contrary motion; strict exercises avoid them.
why 3 0 SIMILAR_MOTION A cadence where every resolution falls: soprano C–B♭, the alto's A drops to F, the seventh E♭ to D and the bass to B♭.
why 4 1/4 LEAPS The bass climbs A–B–C–D–E♭ by step. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 4 1/2 PROGRESSION The bass walks down E♭–D–C–B♭ under the climbing tune; I⁶ is a passing chord on that line, not a step backward.
why 4 3/4 SPACING The tune reaches its high G while the alto stays in the middle on F and E. The gap lasts a beat before the fermata.
why 4 7/8 SPACING The same gap; at the fermata the soprano falls to F and the alto rises to A.
why 5 0 LEADING_TONE The alto's E, leading tone of F, leaps up to A, the third of the F chord at the fermata. The soprano already has F, so rising to F would leave out the third.
why 5 3/4 DOUBLING The bass climbs G–A♭–A–B♭ chromatically, and the diminished chord over A♭ is a passing one. Its doubled F is the soprano's held note.
why 5 3/4 SIX_FOUR A passing chord over the bass's chromatic climb G–A♭–A–B♭, not a real ⁶₄.
why 6 1/2 CROSSING The alto drops a sixth to D, under the tenor's G, to get out of the way of the tune falling to B♭. It climbs back to G two eighths later.
why 6 5/8 CROSSING The same crossing for one more eighth; the alto leaps back up to G on the next beat.
why 6 3/4 LEAPS The alto turns back up after its leap, by a fourth to G rather than a step, and the bass walks down B♭–A–G–F–E by step. Both lines lead into the half cadence on F.
why 7 0 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Soprano and alto fall D–C and G–F a fifth apart, the soprano an eighth earlier. Staggered like this Bach accepts them; strict exercises still count them.
why 7 0 LEAPS After its leap down the tenor steps on to A, the third of the F chord, before turning back up to B♭. It turns one note later than the rule asks.
why 7 1/2 DOUBLING Alto (F–G) and tenor (A–G) meet on G by step from opposite sides.
why 7 5/8 SPACING The alto climbs G–A–B–C in parallel with the soprano while the tenor stays low near the bass. The open space lasts until the fermata.
why 7 3/4 SPACING The same open spacing: alto high on B♮, tenor low on F.
why 8 0 SPACING At the fermata on C minor the gap is still wide; the tenor leaps up an octave right after.
why 8 1/2 UNRESOLVED_NCT The alto strikes B♭ again over the A♭ chord and falls to A♭: a suspension struck anew instead of tied.
why 10 0 SIMILAR_MOTION Every voice rises as the tune leaps up a fifth to F; the others rise to fill in the dominant under it.
why 10 3/4 PROGRESSION The bass circles the dominant, F–F♯–G–F–E–F, under the soprano's E♭–D–E♭–D. The chords on the way are passing ones.
why 11 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION The final cadence: every voice falls into the B♭ chord, the seventh E♭ to D and the bass to B♭.
